  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Michael King shut out the Dodgers for seven innings. One run. That was enough.",
    "what_this_means": "King didn't need help. He needed one moment from the offense to matter, and in the first inning it did. Andujar hit a solo shot. Mason Miller and Jason Adam cleaned up the rest. This is a team that doesn't break when the margin is thin. When the pitching holds. Really holds. They trust it. Four hits and eight strikeouts is not an offensive showcase, but it's also not a liability when you're giving up nothing on the other side. The bullpen held without being tested much. The game stayed 1-0 from the first inning straight through. That's not drama. That's control.",
    "what_to_watch": "What matters is what this win streak is revealing. They've won four straight. They lead the division by a half-game now. The pitching staff has an ERA under four. The formula is working because both sides are clicking at the same time. Not necessarily the offense exploding or the defense turning double plays, but the starter going deep and clean, the pen closing without chaos, and a single moment from the lineup being the entire game. On a night when the bats barely showed, King made silence look like strategy. This is becoming their identity right now. Just enough is enough when nobody gives you more.",
